The default is in `packages/core/src/lib/init.ts`, line 117:

```ts
session: {
  // If no adapter specified, force use of JSON Web Tokens (stateless)
  strategy: config.adapter ? "database" : "jwt",
  ...
  ...config.session,
},
```

This matters for your setup. Passing an adapter flips the default to `"database"`, so your Prisma adapter alone would put sessions back in Postgres. Your JWT sessions only hold if your config explicitly sets `session: { strategy: "jwt" }`. The `...config.session` spread on line 121 means an explicit value wins, so that line is enough. I didn't check your app's config, since it isn't in this repo. It's worth confirming it sets `strategy: "jwt"`, or that a future refactor doesn't drop it.

The throw comes from `serialize()` in `packages/core/src/lib/vendored/cookie.ts` (line 282-285). It checks `options.domain` against `domainValueRegExp` (line 60), which allows only host labels, so `.acme.internal:8443` fails on the `:8443`.

I'd fix it in that same file by stripping the port before validation:

```ts
if (options.domain) {
  const domain = options.domain.replace(/:\d+$/, "")
  if (!domainValueRegExp.test(domain)) {
    throw new TypeError(`option domain is invalid: ${options.domain}`)
  }
  str += "; Domain=" + domain
}
```

Browsers don't include the port in the Domain attribute; cookies are scoped by host, not port. So the port is meaningless here, and stripping it is correct. I would not loosen `domainValueRegExp`, because that would emit an invalid `Domain=` value that browsers ignore. I didn't find where the partner's domain value gets set in config, so if you'd rather normalize it at the source, that would be the other place to look. The vendored serializer is the one file I can confirm needs to change.

